// PerceptualColormaps.swift
// DICOMCore
//
// DICOM Core — the perceptually uniform colormaps, as published data.
//
// GENERATED FILE — do not hand-edit.
//
// Viridis, Inferno, Magma and Plasma by Nathaniel J. Smith, Stefan van der Walt
// and (for Viridis) Eric Firing, released under a CC0 public-domain dedication:
//
//   "This file and the colormaps in it are released under the CC0 license /
//    public domain dedication. We would appreciate credit if you use or
//    redistribute these colormaps, but do not impose any legal restrictions."
//
// Credit is given here as asked. The data is taken from that release
// (github.com/BIDS/colormap) and from nowhere else.
//
// These are worth having on film for one concrete reason: equal steps in the
// data look like equal steps to the eye, so a gradient carries magnitude
// honestly. The spectrum ramps do not have that property — a rainbow invents
// edges where the data is smooth, which on a diagnostic image reads as
// structure that is not there.
//
// All four are published at full 256-entry resolution and are transcribed at
// that resolution: interpolating a handful of anchors would band visibly on
// film, where a gradient can cover a whole sheet.
// NEMA-verified: 2026a, checked 2026-09-25 — carries no DICOM-standard data (CC0 colormap tables). C1 classification confirmed.

import Foundation

/// The four CC0 perceptually uniform colormaps, as transcribed tables.
